# Geography

{{ statement() }}

A metropolitan area is a list of counties, and the list changes. The OMB delineation files committed under `data/delineations` give the county to area mapping for every vintage the window crosses ({{ v('data.omb.vintages') }} vintages, the latest {{ v('data.omb.latest_vintage') }}, with {{ v('data.omb.metropolitan_areas') }} metropolitan and {{ v('data.omb.micropolitan_areas') }} micropolitan areas). Every source is mapped through the vintage in force when it was published, and the warehouse carries each area under its latest code with the earlier codes as aliases.

## How each source is mapped

- **FHFA** publishes by area code and by metropolitan division; the divisions are rolled up to the area through the vintage of the quarter, weighted by owner households (`int_fhfa_area`).
- **ACS** publishes by area code for the vintage of the release year; the code is carried through the alias table to the latest code.
- **Permits and population** are county files; each county is assigned to the area of the vintage in force for that year (`int_county_vintage_map`, `int_county_year`).
- **HMDA** carries the county of the property; the loan is placed through the delineation of its activity year, the derived area code the file also carries is only a check, and a loan whose county is in no area and whose code is blank or the non metro sentinel is flagged by the `metro_unresolved` rule and kept.
- **Puerto Rico** has four metropolitan areas in the delineation with census rows and loans but no FHFA index, so they appear in the explorer's census tables and in no anchored chapter; the warehouse test that asks for a year of prices in every large metropolitan area leaves them out by name.
- **HUD** fair market rents and income limits are published by HUD area; the HUD area to metro map is committed from the HUD area definition file of the latest year (`data/hud_area_map.csv`).

## Tests

Every county maps to exactly one area per vintage (a planted county in two areas is rejected by the `county_in_two_areas` rule and its test); owner household totals reconcile across the {{ v('data.omb.latest_vintage') }} recomposition within the stated tolerance; an HMDA metro code of each year resolves through that year's vintage; every alias points at one latest code. The crosswalk tests live in `tests/data/test_geography.py` and in the warehouse schema.

## What the recomposition moves

The latest delineation renames and recomposes areas. A metro that gained a county between vintages shows a step in its household count that is geography, not growth; the explorer's tables carry the vintage beside the year so the step can be read for what it is, and the chapters that compare years use the anchored index, which FHFA publishes on a fixed area definition, for the price side of every comparison.
